# Line-ending and encoding policy.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (PR-1)
# ---------------------------
# Before this file, the repository had no normalisation policy and relied on
# each developer's `core.autocrlf`. That made byte-level evidence
# unreproducible: ADR 0003's appendix records 15 file digests, 14 of which are
# CRLF worktree digests that cannot be recomputed on an LF checkout
# (see `work/t1-baseline-audit.md` §5 F-1).
#
# PR-1 answers this in two layers:
#   1. EVIDENCE is taken from the object database (`git cat-file blob`), which
#      is LF-normalised regardless of checkout settings. That is what makes
#      digests cross-platform (see `scripts/eval/lib/git-objects.mjs`).
#   2. This file states the intended repository content policy explicitly, so
#      a checkout does not silently differ from the committed bytes.
#
# DELIBERATELY NARROW: this is NOT a blanket `* text=auto` renormalisation.
# A repo-wide renormalise would rewrite every historical blob, invalidate every
# existing digest and create a mass diff with no review value. Only the paths
# below are declared, and each declaration is justified.
#
# ADDING A RULE: state the reason in a comment above it. If a file genuinely
# needs CRLF or a BOM preserved byte-for-byte, mark it `-text` so git never
# converts it and the fixture stays a true binary-equivalent artefact.

# --- Source, tests, scripts, docs: normalise to LF on commit ---------------
# These are the only files that enter the PR-1 composite digest, so pinning
# them to LF is what keeps `path:sha256` lines identical on every platform.
*.ts    text eol=lf
*.mts   text eol=lf
*.cts   text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.cjs   text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf

# --- Shell / CI entry points: LF is mandatory -----------------------------
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.bash  text eol=lf

# --- Windows-only scripts: CRLF is the correct working-tree form ----------
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# --- Binary-equivalent: never convert, never diff as text -----------------
# Byte-exact fixtures (any future golden files, archives, images, fonts).
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.docx  binary
*.xlsx  binary

# --- Explicit BOM / CRLF fixtures ----------------------------------------
# `-text` means "no conversion in either direction": the bytes in the worktree
# are the bytes in the object database, so a BOM or CRLF fixture survives.
# Add such fixtures under `tests/fixtures/encoding/` and list them here.
tests/fixtures/encoding/** -text
